WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.260
In the game of thrones, you win, or you die,
and a lot of fans apparently saw their hopes

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.140
for a good ending go down in flames with the
series finale.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.340
What did people have to say about the series'
controversial conclusion?

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.460
Here are some of the more searing reactions.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:18.860
Tragic, ironic, and maybe inevitable, Jon
Snow ended his arc not sitting the Iron Throne,

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.860
but instead as the slayer of both his kin
and his queen.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:25.700
Jon's stabbing of Daenerys in the destroyed
throne room of the Red Keep is exactly the

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.380
kind of story turn we've come to expect from
Game of Thrones.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.980
But the turns it took to get there felt pretty
rushed to a lot of people.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.020
"So what happened to her?"

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:34.940
"Well—"

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:35.500
"The quick version."

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:38.719
One poster expressed sympathy for Daenerys
as a character, writing,

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:42.749
"a moment of silence for daenerys targaryen,
a victim to horrible writing and a rushed

00:00:42.749 --> 00:00:43.240
story.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:44.700
may she rest in peace"

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.720
Others took the conclusion of her story personally,
with more than one poster expressing real

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:49.620
frustration.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.020
According to one commenter,

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:53.220
"daenerys deserves SUCH a better death.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.329
after all she’s done, good and bad, she’s
one of the most iconic characters in television

00:00:57.329 --> 00:01:00.600
history...and that death did not do her justice
AT ALL.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.180
I’M MAD."

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.900
Even people who liked the finale seemed to
express some frustration with the speed at

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.500
which Daenerys' story developed.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.740
"Initial #GameOfThronesFinale thoughts: An
excellent finale.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.030
I still think Dany's decisions were rushed
but the outcome was satisfying and there were

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:17.080
strong character moments for just about everyone,
especially Tyrion."

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:21.260
Some wondered just where Dany's guards were
during her final encounter with Jon, who was

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.980
able to easily stab the queen during a private
moment, at a time when the loyalty of her

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.140
advisors was actively being questioned.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.360
As one writer put it,

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.660
"why did Tyrion have more guards then Daenerys?

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.960
Why did they take Jons weapon away when he
saw Tyrion but not Dany?"

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.820
One commenter broke it down as follows:

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:36.980
"That was terrible.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:41.180
Like she'd just wander off to the throne room
alone without some Unsullied or Dothraki guarding

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.050
her, especially from the likes of Jon Snow,
whom she said betrayed her and was the biggest

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.320
threat to her claim to the throne."

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:51.140
Others outright rejected the idea that Daenerys
would have gone so mad so fast, still feeling

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:54.170
the sting of her sudden heel turn in the previous
episode, "The Bells."

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:58.010
Immediately after the finale, the overall
impression was that even if her turn to villany

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:01.000
could have worked, the story felt just too
sped up.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:05.260
One of the most surprising outcomes of the
Game of Thrones finale involved the out-of-nowhere

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:09.380
crowning of Bran Stark as king of the seven,
scratch that, six, kingdoms.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.460
Twitter had a field day with the unexpected
turn, and plenty of viewers were gobsmacked

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.560
by the idea that it was even happening at
all.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:16.880
In the words of one critic,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.700
"So Bran gets to be king, despite the fact
that he just put his name on the group project

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:21.700
and didn’t do any work."

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:25.760
Others found humor in the idea that Bran,
with his ability to see the future, has apparently

00:02:25.769 --> 00:02:29.769
been waiting patiently for everything to be
put into place so that he might take the throne.

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:30.740
As one commenter said,

00:02:30.909 --> 00:02:34.760
"So for like 6 seasons, Bran been in his room
singing 'I Just Can’t Wait to Be King' to

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:35.260
himself."

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:39.320
Some posters got nostalgic for the early days
of the show, and pointed out once again how

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:43.200
consequential Jaime Lannister's actions were
when he first pushed Bran from a Winterfell

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:43.700
window.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.060
In the words of one writer,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.360
"Jamie’s [kingslaying] level is so high
he tried to kill Bran 8 years in advance"

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.740
And of course, plenty of people had jokes
to share about Bran's wheelchair taking the

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.300
place of the Iron Throne, with one poster
writing,

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.600
"Dany broke the wheel and ended up with two
more"

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.439
As for Isaac Hempstead Wright himself, all
the actor had to share on Twitter in the wake

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.460
of the finale on Twitter was a simple crown
emoji and a shrug of the shoulders.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:09.779
One other big development of the finale was
the conclusion of Arya Stark's story, with

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.880
the tomboy royal family member evolving from
desperate survivor to trained assassin, and

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.700
finally to an explorer of the worlds beyond
Westeros.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:18.240
Was it a satisfying end?

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:19.660
One writer asked,

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:22.740
"So who had “Arya invents colonialism”
in their end of show pool"

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.840
Others saw sequel story potential, with one
poster writing,

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.320
'I’d watch an Arya Goes West spin-off just
saying."

00:03:28.409 --> 00:03:32.379
Others couldn't help but see dramatic similarities
between the ending of Game of Thrones and

00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:33.819
the ending of The Lord of the Rings.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:34.860
As one observer noted,

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:38.769
"Remember when they told us how different
#GameOfThones was from Lord of the Rings and

00:03:38.769 --> 00:03:41.640
how it subverted everything ever...Arya goes
into the west.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.140
Sam writes There and Back Again.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:44.480
Coooooool."

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:48.900
During Game of Thrones final episodes, much
has been made of the idea of character assassination.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:53.140
Jaime seemingly throwing away years of growth
to go be bad with his sister one last time;

00:03:53.159 --> 00:03:57.180
Daenerys throwing away years of stately ambition
and just going full rampage mode on King's

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:57.680
Landing.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.779
It's impossible to deny that plenty has happened
to cause many fans, and even the series' actors,

00:04:02.779 --> 00:04:04.579
to wonder what their characters are thinking.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:09.280
"...they've put the women and children in
a crypt with all the dead people, so… blah.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.000
Tyrion is smart, but I guess not that smart."

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.459
But no instance of out-of-character behavior
so incensed viewers as Jon Snow's cold farewell

00:04:15.459 --> 00:04:18.370
to his loyal direwolf, Ghost, in episode four.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:22.250
Fans were outraged that Jon, who had the idea
for the Stark children to adopt the direwolves

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:26.140
in the first place, would so callously send
his dog away after years of sacrifice and

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:27.300
fighting along his side.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:30.980
The poor boy lost an ear for the one-time
King in the North, and he didn't even pet

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:31.740
him goodbye.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:32.900
What a royal jerk!

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.780
"To the traitor and bastard Jon Snow."

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.980
But all of this was forgiven when the finale
had know-nothing Jon Snow finally smile, kneel

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:42.340
down, and just give a good pet to his loyal
dog.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.810
Responding to the backlash, series director
David Nutter initially said the two hadn't

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.560
interacted during their goodbye thanks to
the difficulty of rendering the CGI direwolf,

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.440
an excuse which many took as being kind of
a stretch.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:57.720
When Jon ultimately did embrace the wolf during
the finale, one poster reacted with relief,

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:58.220
writing,

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:01.860
"Game of Thrones somehow found the money to
allow Jon Snow to pet his dog Ghost."

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.170
Another writer cheekily proposed the idea
that the scene reeked of a quick fix, saying,

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:09.640
"they totally shot that Ghost scene on like
Wednesday afternoon, didn't they"

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:13.880
Others made it seem as though this one moment
single-handedly redeemed the series, despite

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:15.640
any perceived late-game flaws.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:17.140
According to one commenter,

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:19.540
"Good boy Ghost finally got the love he deserved.

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.280
My happy ending for #GameOfThrones."

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.420
Plenty of other commenters just used the occasion
to post their favorite animated .gifs of dogs

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:26.300
and direwolves.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:30.580
The Game of Thrones finale may be contentious,
but beloved pets deserving affection is one

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:32.420
thing it seems every viewer can agree on.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:36.920
As Game of Thrones came to a close, some were
incensed not only at the final episode, but

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:39.800
all the events that led up to it during the
final season as a whole.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:43.740
To these critics, the finale was a capstone
on many episodes' worth of questionable writing

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.380
and bad creative decisions.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.820
Speaking generally, YouTuber Jacksepticeye
wrote,

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.040
"Game of Thrones feels like the showrunners
knew where they wanted the characters to end

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:53.440
up but had no idea how to get them there this
season.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:55.700
It felt incredibly rushed and ham fisted.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:57.800
What a waste of amazing lore and world building."

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.980
Another writer criticized the plot developments
in general with a searing summary, tweeting,

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.850
"so we waited 2 years for
- the night king to be stabbed in his crop

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:08.220
top and die easier than the mountain
- cersei to get trampled by rocks

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:11.050
- jon to stab daenerys only for BRAN to become
king

00:06:11.050 --> 00:06:14.480
- [and] jon to [fly] back to nights watch
when there r no white walkers??"

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.320
Summing up her own disappointment, author
Madeleine Roux said,

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.820
"I'd just like to point out that the last
spoken words of Game of Thrones were: I once

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:27.680
brought a jackass and a honeycomb into a brothel."

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.280
She followed it up with a subsequent tweet,
saying,

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:33.460
"I can't BELIEVE I have to clarify this but:
This is not a good thing."

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:37.860
In the hours since the series ended, these
takes have been accompanied by many, many

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:41.650
others, commenting on a finale that seems
destined to be a point of contention for years

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:42.160
to come.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:46.160
But whether people are outraged, resigned,
or genuinely okay with how the series wrapped

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:49.480
up, the story is finally over, and now our
watch has ended.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.820
Best of luck with that prequel series, HBO.

